{
  "version": 358,
  "released": "2026-09-25",
  "changelog": "v358: a gendered word when it is known \u2014 son or daughter, father or mother, and the neutral word when nobody has said which. THE RULING (owner, 2026-09-25), verbatim: \"we can use both child and son, I in life have four children all who are my child or children and I have 2 sons and 2 daughters so both\" and \"son is more detailed than child and gives more information, so if you have to pick one i'd pick the gendered one daughter and son\"; and the scope, the same day: every relation whose word HAS a gendered form, and a card for no other (\"each person isn't my child, so is this really for everyone?\"). WHAT WAS MISSING: the roster holds a relationship from a closed set (parent, child, sibling, spouse, grandparent, \u2026) and nothing about which FORM of the word the owner uses, so \"Son\" was unavailable, not suppressed. THE FIELD: one per person, `relation_gender` \u2208 `male`|`female`|`neutral` on the roster row (+ `relation_gender_basis`), the grammatical gender of the word he uses \u2014 `neutral` is his answer \"keep 'child'\". Every word is DERIVED from (relationship, relation_gender) by `relation_words.relation_word`: Son/Daughter, Father/Mother, Brother/Sister, Husband/Wife, Grandfather/Grandmother. A gender rather than a stored word because a stored \"son\" duplicates `relationship`, can disagree with it, and would need re-asking after every relationship correction. The pairs come out of `identity_resolution.RELATIONSHIP_MENTION_WORDS` (the neutral word's set minus `FOCUS_RELATIONSHIPS`); the one declaration is `WORD_GENDER`. Both fields join `entity_roster._SETTLED_IDENTITY_FIELDS`. WHERE IT COMES FROM (`THE_FIELD_IS_SET_ONLY_BY_THE_OWNERS_OWN_WORDS`): his answer to the card (basis `answer`, `record_relation_gender`, always wins), else \u2014 read fresh on every publish, never written \u2014 a gendered word he used about that person: a roster spelling that IS the word (\"dad\", \"my mother\", \"A.J. (brother)\"), a `family` landmark `relation`, or a v346/v347 introduction in his own tellings via `roster_relations.relationship_phrase_match`, so a possessor (\"my dad's dad\") and an in-law/compound (\"mother-in-law\", \"step-mother\") are refused exactly as there; the word must belong to the row's own relationship; a spelling two people share anchors nobody; never a first name. RENDERING (`A_GENDERED_WORD_WHEN_IT_IS_KNOWN`): the projection publishes `relation_words` (served by `calculated_view`) \u2014 per roster person with a relationship: subject_ref, spellings, the neutral and gendered word and label, and the neutral plural; a collective row (\"Kids\") reads \"Children\". No framework seat rendered a roster relationship label before this (the platform person page reads a `frontmatter.relationship` the framework never wrote), so this is the one published seat. ASKING (`A_CARD_ONLY_WHERE_THE_WORD_HAS_A_GENDERED_PAIR`): new work-item kind `relation_word` (registered in `temporal_projection`/`timeline_interaction` WORK_ITEM_KINDS, a probe, `SURFACES_BY_KIND` = timeline only, `WORK_ITEM_VALUE_DEFAULTS`, placement gain 0; requested field `relation_gender` is canonical in `temporal_work_items`) \u2014 at most one per real named person whose relationship has a gendered pair and whose word is unknown, never for an alias row, a collective or role row, the owner, or a friend/colleague/mentor/partner/other; no node, no leverage, combined_score 0.115 (below every dating card); options published as `candidates`. Asked on the Timeline, never on a person page. The reply files through the ordinary card-answer path (`answer_placement.place_answers`, `landmark_recorder.file_claims`) into the roster field and the card is never minted again. ON THE OWNER'S VAULT (read-only rig): 17 roster rows \u2014 child 2, parent 2, sibling 1 (+1 alias row), spouse 1, grandparent 1, no relationship 9; 5 of the 7 relatives get their word from his own words with no card (Brother, Mother, Father, Grandfather, Wife); 2 cards minted, both `child`: \"Is Harvey your son or your daughter \u2014 or would you rather keep \u201cchild\u201d?\" and the same for James Everett Taylor; cards by kind 64 \u2192 66 (on v357's identity fix) (+2 relation_word, every other kind unchanged); nodes 1265 \u2192 1265, placed 1219 \u2192 1219, zero nodes changed, lost-placement audit empty; answering Harvey's card \"He is my son\" on the rig filed `male` and his label became Son and the card left. Charlee and Dottie have no roster row, so no label and no card until they do. `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION` DOES NOT MOVE: no date arithmetic, node or placement changes; this is a publication-time display seam (`temporal_publication._with_relation_words`, applied in publish and verify), and `relation_words.RELATION_WORD_RULE_VERSION` joins the publication fingerprint so upgraded vaults re-derive. HOSTS: the platform must pin v358, register `relation_word` in `framework_shapes.WORK_ITEM_KINDS`, accept the served `relation_words` view key, and have the person page read its word from `relation_words` (match `subject_ref` or a spelling) before `frontmatter.relationship`. GUARDS: `tests/test_v358_a_gendered_word_when_it_is_known.py` (known \u2192 Son, unknown \u2192 Child, plural \u2192 children, \"my dad's dad\" never father, \"mother-in-law\" never mother, a friend gets no card, an alias row never gets a card, an answered card never re-mints, and the live and sweep answer seats), each guard mutation-checked.",
